    <hr size="1" />
    <div id="sC-component-footnotes" class="ts-footnote-2">
<p><a name="footnote1"></a><sup>1</sup> See Williams (2018).</p>
<p><a name="footnote2"></a><sup>2</sup> See Mertens and Williams (2019, 2020). Note that the presence of the lower  bound implies the existence of two deterministic steady states. For the present  purposes, I focus exclusively on the case of the &ldquo;target equilibrium&quot; as  described in Mertens and Williams (forthcoming).</p>
<p><a name="footnote3"></a><sup>3</sup> Inflation is determined by a Phillips curve by which inflation increases with  the output gap and expectations about future inflation as well as a supply  shock. The output gap is linked to the real interest rate relative to the  natural rate of interest and expectations of the future output gap as well as a  demand shock. Absent a lower bound on interest rates, the central bank follows  a textbook inflation-targeting policy. See Mertens (2020) for more details.</p>
<p><a name="footnote4"></a><sup>4</sup> Note that the same conclusions apply to the output gap as well, although the magnitude  of this effect on the mean level of the output gap is much smaller than that  for inflation, based on typical parameter values for models such as this.</p>
<p><a name="footnote5"></a><sup>5</sup> See, for example, Evans (2010), Bernanke (2017), Bernanke, Kiley, and Roberts  (2019), Svensson (2020), and Amano et al (2020). Mertens and Williams (2019,  2020) provide an analysis and comparison of a range of alternative policies.</p>
<p><a name="footnote6"></a><sup>6</sup> Note that not all of these proposed policies ensure that the mean inflation  rate equals the target level, as discussed in Mertens and Williams (2020). In  particular, policy rules that explicitly target a multi-period average of  inflation that is relatively short may not fully eradicate the downward bias to  inflation expectations owing to the lower bound. The reasoning is the same as  for inflation targeting: the overshoot of inflation may be inadequate to fully  offset the undershoot of the target when policy is constrained. By comparison,  as shown in Mertens and Williams (2020), price-level targeting and the  Reifschneider-Williams rule deliver average inflation and inflation  expectations at the target level.</p>
<p><a name="footnote7"></a><sup>7</sup> See Mertens and Williams (2019, 2020).</p>
<p><a name="footnote8"></a><sup>8</sup> See Mertens and Williams (2020).</p>
<p><a name="footnote9"></a><sup>9</sup> Outside of the period of the shock, we take the expectations of each variable,  taking account all possible realizations of the shock. Note that for these  simulations, we use the parameter values from Mertens and Williams (2019,  2020). </p>
<p><a name="footnote10"></a><sup>10</sup> Reifschneider and Williams (2000).</p>
<p><a name="footnote11"></a><sup>11</sup> Mertens and Williams (2020) provides these comparisons.</p>
<p><a name="footnote12"></a><sup>12</sup> Interestingly, under the RW rule, the inflation rate rises above target in the  period of the demand shock, due to expectations of higher inflation in the next  period. However, as seen in Mertens and Williams (2020), this is not a general  feature of dynamic AIT policies, for which inflation may be below target during  the period of the shock.</p>
<p><a name="footnote13"></a><sup>13</sup> The same is true for supply shocks, as shown in Mertens and Williams (2020).</p>    </div>
         

